\title{The Median as a Metric Projection:\\ Firm Nonexpansiveness, a Characterisation,\\ and Iterated Median Filters}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{21 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The median of three numbers is the canonical robust summary of a small ensemble of
measurements, and its robustness is classically expressed by a \emph{qualitative} bracketing
statement: a single corrupted measurement cannot push the median outside the interval spanned
by the two clean ones. We replace this by a complete quantitative theory, obtained by
identifying the object the median actually is. Holding two ``clean'' seeds $a,b$ fixed and
letting the third seed $x$ vary, the median $\med(x,a,b)$ coincides with the metric projection
$P_{[\alpha,\beta]}(x)$ of $x$ onto the bracket $[\alpha,\beta]=[\min(a,b),\max(a,b)]$.
Consequently the sharp robustness constant is not $1$ (nonexpansiveness) but the \emph{firm}
inequality
\[
(Tx-Ty)^2 + \bigl((x-Tx)-(y-Ty)\bigr)^2 \le (x-y)^2,
\]
a Pythagorean budget shared by the displacement of the estimate and the displacement of the
residual --- the part of the corruption the median absorbs. We prove this together with the
variational inequality, the Pythagorean inequality and uniqueness of the nearest point, so the
median is exhibited as the proximal map of the bracket's indicator. We then close the
characterisation problem: on the line, firm nonexpansiveness is exactly monotonicity plus
$1$-Lipschitzness, equivalently nonexpansiveness of the reflection $2T-I$; hence a self-map of
the line is the median-as-projection if and only if it is firmly nonexpansive, fixes the two
endpoints of the bracket and has range in the bracket, and firmness cannot be weakened to
nonexpansiveness (witness $x\mapsto\min(|x|,1)$). We locate what is one-dimensional: closure of
firmness under composition holds on the line and fails in $\R^2$, whereas firmness itself lifts
to $\R^n$, where the coordinatewise median is the Euclidean projection onto a box. Perturbing
all seeds simultaneously, the median is $\ell^\infty$-$1$-Lipschitz, as is every rung of the
associated quota ladder, so Lipschitz robustness does not separate order statistics --- only
breakdown does. Finally, relaxed median updates contract the residual by exactly $(1-\lambda)$
per step, and unrelaxed iteration of any firmly nonexpansive self-map of the line with a fixed
point converges, giving convergent alternating median filters with no spurious compromise limits.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} median; metric projection; firm nonexpansiveness; variational
inequality; proximal operator; Krasnoselskii--Mann iteration; alternating projections; order
statistics; breakdown point.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The setting}

An experimental pipeline produces an \emph{ensemble} of measurements of a single scalar
quantity. Our motivating instance is the \emph{knee} of a performance curve: the value of a
control parameter at which returns begin to diminish. Because the estimate is noisy, the
experiment is repeated with several random seeds, producing an ensemble $K=(K_i)_{i\in\iota}$
of candidate knees, and one number must be reported.

Reporting the mean is unacceptable: a single divergent run destroys it. The standard remedy is
an \emph{order statistic}, most commonly the median, and the standard justification is a
breakdown argument: with three seeds, one arbitrary corruption cannot move the median outside
the interval spanned by the two uncorrupted seeds,
\begin{equation}
\min(a,b)\ \le\ \med(x,a,b)\ \le\ \max(a,b)\qquad\text{for all }x.
\label{eq:bracket}
\end{equation}
Statement~\eqref{eq:bracket} is qualitative: it bounds the damage from an arbitrarily large
corruption but says nothing about the response to a small one. The practically urgent question
is the modulus of continuity.

\subsection{The observation}

Write
\[
\med(x,a,b) = \max\bigl(\min(x,a),\ \min(\max(x,a),b)\bigr)
\]
for the middle of three reals. Fix $a$ and $b$ and vary $x$: then $\med(\cdot,a,b)$ is the
\emph{clamp} of $x$ into $[\min(a,b),\max(a,b)]$, returning $x$ if $x$ lies inside and the
nearer endpoint otherwise. On the line, the clamp is precisely the metric projection onto the
interval. Thus the median, apparently a combinatorial selection rule, is an instance of the most
studied operator class in convex analysis, and the variational inequality, the Pythagorean
inequality, uniqueness of the nearest point, firm nonexpansiveness, the reflection calculus and
the convergence theory of projection algorithms all transfer verbatim.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em]
  \item \textbf{Identification} (\S\ref{sec:proj}): the three-seed median is the metric
    projection onto the bracket spanned by the two remaining seeds.
  \item \textbf{Firm nonexpansiveness} (\S\ref{sec:firm}): the median satisfies a Pythagorean
    robustness budget; nonexpansiveness is a corollary. The median is the unique nearest point
    of the bracket, hence the proximal map of its indicator.
  \item \textbf{Characterisation} (\S\ref{sec:char}): on $\R$, firm nonexpansiveness $=$
    monotone $+$ $1$-Lipschitz $=$ nonexpansiveness of the reflection; three axioms determine
    the median, and firmness cannot be weakened.
  \item \textbf{What is one-dimensional} (\S\ref{sec:dim}): composition-closure is a theorem
    about the line and fails in $\R^2$; firmness itself lifts to $\R^n$.
  \item \textbf{Simultaneous perturbation} (\S\ref{sec:linf}): $\ell^\infty$-$1$-Lipschitzness
    of the median and of every rung of the quota ladder.
  \item \textbf{Iteration} (\S\ref{sec:iter}): exact geometric decay for relaxed updates; an
    order-theoretic unrelaxed convergence theorem; convergent alternating median filters.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Notation and standing definitions}

Throughout, $T$ is a self-map of $\R$ (or $\R^n$ where indicated), $I$ the identity, $I-T$ the
\emph{residual}.

\begin{definition}[Median of three]
For $x,a,b\in\R$, $\med(x,a,b):=\max\bigl(\min(x,a),\min(\max(x,a),b)\bigr)$, the middle value
of the multiset $\{x,a,b\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Clamp / projection onto an interval]
For $a\le b$ and $x\in\R$, $P_{[a,b]}(x):=\max\bigl(a,\min(x,b)\bigr)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Firm nonexpansiveness]\label{def:firm}
$T:\R\to\R$ is \emph{firmly nonexpansive} if for all $x,y$,
\begin{equation}
(Tx-Ty)^2+\bigl((x-Tx)-(y-Ty)\bigr)^2\ \le\ (x-y)^2 .
\label{eq:firm}
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Nonexpansiveness]
$T$ is \emph{nonexpansive} if $|Tx-Ty|\le|x-y|$ for all $x,y$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Monotone $1$-Lipschitz, increment form]
$T$ is \emph{monotone $1$-Lipschitz} if $x\le y$ implies $Tx\le Ty$ and $Ty-Tx\le y-x$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Firmness in $\R^n$; box projection]\label{def:firmn}
With $\lVert v\rVert^2=\sum_i v_i^2$, a map $T:\R^n\to\R^n$ is firmly nonexpansive if
$\lVert Tx-Ty\rVert^2+\lVert (x-Tx)-(y-Ty)\rVert^2\le\lVert x-y\rVert^2$ for all $x,y$. For
$a_i\le b_i$ the \emph{box projection} is $(P_{[a,b]}x)_i:=P_{[a_i,b_i]}(x_i)$, i.e.\ the
coordinatewise median.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Quota ladder]
For a finite ensemble $K:\iota\to\N$ and a quota $m\le|\iota|$, the \emph{quota budget} $Q(K,m)$
is the largest $b$ such that at least $m$ indices satisfy $K_i\ge b$; equivalently the $m$-th
largest entry. The family $\{Q(K,m)\}_m$ is the \emph{quota ladder}; its middle rung is the
median.
\end{definition}

\section{The median is a metric projection}\label{sec:proj}

\begin{lemma}[Clamp $=$ median for ordered endpoints]\label{lem:clamp}
For $a\le b$ and all $x$, $P_{[a,b]}(x)=\med(x,a,b)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both sides are piecewise linear in $x$ with breakpoints at $a$ and $b$. For $x\le a$ both equal
$a$; for $a\le x\le b$ both equal $x$; for $x\ge b$ both equal $b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The median is a projection]\label{thm:medproj}
For all $a,b,x\in\R$, $\med(x,a,b)=P_{[\min(a,b),\max(a,b)]}(x)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:clamp} to the ordered pair $\min(a,b)\le\max(a,b)$; the right side becomes
$\med\bigl(x,\min(a,b),\max(a,b)\bigr)$, which equals $\med(x,a,b)$ since the median is symmetric
in its arguments. Alternatively, split on the six orderings of $x,a,b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Elementary properties]\label{prop:basic}
Let $a\le b$ and $P=P_{[a,b]}$. Then \textup{(1)} $P$ is monotone; \textup{(2)} $Px\in[a,b]$;
\textup{(3)} $Px=x$ for $x\in[a,b]$, so $P$ is idempotent and $\Fix P=[a,b]$; \textup{(4)}
$Px=a$ for $x\le a$ and $Px=b$ for $x\ge b$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\max$ and $\min$ are monotone in each argument, giving (1). For (2), $Px\ge a$ by the outer
$\max$, and $Px\le b$ since $a\le b$ and $\min(x,b)\le b$. Items (3)--(4) are direct evaluations.
\end{proof}

\section{Firm nonexpansiveness}\label{sec:firm}

\subsection{Algebraic normal form}

\begin{lemma}[Inner-product form]\label{lem:inner}
$T$ is firmly nonexpansive if and only if $(Tx-Ty)^2\le(Tx-Ty)(x-y)$ for all $x,y$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Put $u=Tx-Ty$, $v=x-y$. Then \eqref{eq:firm} reads $u^2+(v-u)^2\le v^2$, i.e.\ $2u^2\le 2uv$.
\end{proof}

In Hilbert-space language this is $\langle Tx-Ty,\,x-y\rangle\ge\lVert Tx-Ty\rVert^2$.

\subsection{The variational inequality}

\begin{theorem}[Variational inequality; obtuse angle]\label{thm:var}
Let $a\le b$, $x\in\R$ and $y\in[a,b]$. Then $(x-P_{[a,b]}x)(y-P_{[a,b]}x)\le 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $x\le a$ then $Px=a$, so the first factor is $x-a\le0$ and the second $y-a\ge0$. If
$x\in[a,b]$ then $Px=x$ and the first factor vanishes. If $x\ge b$ then $Px=b$, the first factor
is $x-b\ge0$ and the second $y-b\le0$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The headline theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Firm nonexpansiveness of the median-as-projection]\label{thm:firm}
Let $a\le b$. Then $P=P_{[a,b]}$ is firmly nonexpansive:
\[
(Px-Py)^2+\bigl((x-Px)-(y-Py)\bigr)^2\ \le\ (x-y)^2\qquad\text{for all }x,y.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:inner} it suffices to show $(Px-Py)^2\le(Px-Py)(x-y)$. Apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:var} at $x$ with test point $Py\in[a,b]$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:basic}(2)),
obtaining $(x-Px)(Py-Px)\le0$, and again at $y$ with test point $Px$, obtaining
$(y-Py)(Px-Py)\le0$. Adding and expanding gives the claim. This is verbatim the classical
Hilbert-space argument.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The perturbation $x\mapsto y$ has a fixed quadratic budget $(x-y)^2$, split between the motion
of the reported estimate and the motion of the absorbed residual $x-Px$. Output motion is paid
for by absorption in quadrature.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}[Nonexpansiveness]\label{cor:ne}
Every firmly nonexpansive $T$ satisfies $|Tx-Ty|\le|x-y|$. In particular the median moves by at
most the perturbation of a single seed.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Discard the nonnegative residual term in \eqref{eq:firm} and take square roots.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Nearest point, Pythagoras, uniqueness}

\begin{theorem}[Pythagorean inequality]\label{thm:pyth}
For $a\le b$, $x\in\R$ and $y\in[a,b]$: $(x-Px)^2+(Px-y)^2\le(x-y)^2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand $(x-y)^2=(x-Px)^2+(Px-y)^2+2(x-Px)(Px-y)$ and note that Theorem~\ref{thm:var} gives
$(x-Px)(Px-y)\ge0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Nearest point]
$|x-Px|\le|x-y|$ for all $y\in[a,b]$: the median realises the distance to the bracket.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness of the minimiser]\label{thm:unique}
If $y\in[a,b]$ and $(x-y)^2\le(x-Px)^2$, then $y=Px$. Hence $Px$ is the unique minimiser of
$y\mapsto|x-y|$ over $[a,b]$, and the median is the proximal map of the indicator
$\iota_{[a,b]}$:
\[
P_{[a,b]}(x)=\prox_{\iota_{[a,b]}}(x)=\operatorname*{arg\,min}_{y}\ \tfrac12(x-y)^2+\iota_{[a,b]}(y).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Combine the hypothesis with Theorem~\ref{thm:pyth} to get $(Px-y)^2\le0$.
\end{proof}

\section{The characterisation}\label{sec:char}

\subsection{Firmness on the line is an order condition}

\begin{theorem}[Characterisation of firmness on $\R$]\label{thm:monolip}
$T:\R\to\R$ is firmly nonexpansive if and only if it is monotone and $1$-Lipschitz in increment
form: $x\le y$ implies $Tx\le Ty$ and $Ty-Tx\le y-x$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) Lemma~\ref{lem:inner} at the pair $(y,x)$ with $x\le y$ gives
$(Ty-Tx)^2\le(Ty-Tx)(y-x)$. If $Ty-Tx<0$, dividing by this negative quantity yields
$Ty-Tx\ge y-x\ge0$, a contradiction; so $T$ is monotone. Given monotonicity, if $Ty-Tx>0$ divide
by it to get $Ty-Tx\le y-x$; if $Ty=Tx$ the bound is trivial. ($\Leftarrow$) For $x\le y$ both
$Ty-Tx$ and $(y-x)-(Ty-Tx)$ are nonnegative; their product being nonnegative is exactly
$(Ty-Tx)^2\le(Ty-Tx)(y-x)$. The case $y\le x$ is symmetric.
\end{proof}

This theorem is the pivot of the paper: it converts a \emph{metric} hypothesis into an
\emph{order} hypothesis. No such collapse occurs in a general Hilbert space.

\begin{theorem}[Reflection form]\label{thm:refl}
$T$ is firmly nonexpansive if and only if the reflection $R=2T-I$ is nonexpansive; equivalently,
$T=\tfrac12(I+R)$ is the midpoint average of the identity and a nonexpansive map.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$(Rx-Ry)^2=4(Tx-Ty)^2-4(Tx-Ty)(x-y)+(x-y)^2$, so $(Rx-Ry)^2\le(x-y)^2$ is equivalent to
$(Tx-Ty)^2\le(Tx-Ty)(x-y)$, i.e.\ Lemma~\ref{lem:inner}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Order-convex fixed-point sets]\label{prop:fix}
If $T$ is firmly nonexpansive, $Tu=u$, $Tv=v$ and $u\le w\le v$, then $Tw=w$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:monolip} on the pair $u\le w$: $Tw-u=Tw-Tu\le w-u$, so $Tw\le w$. On the
pair $w\le v$: $v-Tw=Tv-Tw\le v-w$, so $Tw\ge w$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The median is determined by three axioms}

\begin{theorem}[Characterisation of the median-as-projection]\label{thm:char}
Fix $a\le b$ and let $T:\R\to\R$. Then $T=P_{[a,b]}$ if and only if
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*),leftmargin=2.2em]
  \item $T$ is firmly nonexpansive;
  \item $Ta=a$ and $Tb=b$;
  \item $Tx\in[a,b]$ for every $x$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) Theorem~\ref{thm:firm} and Proposition~\ref{prop:basic}. ($\Leftarrow$) Rewrite
(i) as monotone $+$ $1$-Lipschitz (Theorem~\ref{thm:monolip}). If $x\le a$, monotonicity gives
$Tx\le Ta=a$ while (iii) gives $Tx\ge a$, so $Tx=a$. If $x\ge b$, symmetrically $Tx=b$. If
$a\le x\le b$, the Lipschitz bound on $[a,x]$ gives $Tx-a\le x-a$ and on $[x,b]$ gives
$b-Tx\le b-x$, so $Tx=x$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Firmness is necessary: a nonexpansive impostor}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]\label{thm:sharp}
The map $T(x)=\min(|x|,1)$ is nonexpansive, has range $[0,1]$ and fixed-point set exactly
$[0,1]$ --- the same as $P_{[0,1]}$ --- yet $T\ne P_{[0,1]}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Nonexpansiveness: $u\mapsto\min(u,1)$ is a clamp, hence $1$-Lipschitz by
Corollary~\ref{cor:ne}, and $\bigl||x|-|y|\bigr|\le|x-y|$; compose. Range: $0\le\min(|x|,1)\le1$.
Fixed points: $Tx=x$ forces $x\in[0,1]$, and conversely $x\in[0,1]$ gives $\min(x,1)=x$. Finally
$T(-2)=1\ne 0=P_{[0,1]}(-2)$.
\end{proof}

The impostor fails exactly monotonicity, hence firmness --- and its failure is qualitatively
catastrophic: it reports a seed far \emph{below} the bracket as though it were far \emph{above}
it. The Pythagorean inequality \eqref{eq:firm} is therefore load-bearing in
Theorem~\ref{thm:char}.

\section{What is one-dimensional and what is not}\label{sec:dim}

\begin{theorem}[Closure under composition on $\R$]\label{thm:comp}
If $S,T:\R\to\R$ are firmly nonexpansive, so is $S\circ T$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:monolip} firmness is monotone $+$ $1$-Lipschitz, and both classes are closed
under composition: for $x\le y$, $Tx\le Ty$ hence $S(Tx)\le S(Ty)$, and
$S(Ty)-S(Tx)\le Ty-Tx\le y-x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Failure in $\R^2$]\label{thm:compfail}
Let $P_1(u,v)=(u,0)$ (projection onto the horizontal axis) and
$P_2(u,v)=\bigl(\tfrac{u+v}{2},\tfrac{u+v}{2}\bigr)$ (projection onto the diagonal). Both are
firmly nonexpansive in the sense of Definition~\ref{def:firmn}, but $P_1\circ P_2$ is not.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $P_1$, writing $x-y=(s,t)$, the left-hand side equals $s^2+t^2$ exactly. For $P_2$,
$\lVert P_2x-P_2y\rVert^2=\tfrac{(s+t)^2}{2}$ and
$\lVert (I-P_2)x-(I-P_2)y\rVert^2=\tfrac{(s-t)^2}{2}$, summing to $s^2+t^2$ exactly (as for any
orthogonal projection). For the composite take $x=(0,1)$, $y=(0,0)$, $T=P_1\circ P_2$: then
$Tx=(\tfrac12,0)$, $Ty=(0,0)$, so $\lVert Tx-Ty\rVert^2=\tfrac14$ and
$(x-Tx)-(y-Ty)=(-\tfrac12,1)$ has squared norm $\tfrac54$. The total $\tfrac32$ exceeds
$\lVert x-y\rVert^2=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Firmness of the coordinatewise median in $\R^n$]\label{thm:box}
Let $a_i\le b_i$ for all $i$. The box projection $P_{[a,b]}$ of Definition~\ref{def:firmn} ---
the coordinatewise median --- is firmly nonexpansive for the Euclidean norm on $\R^n$, and
realises the Euclidean distance to the box: $\lVert x-P_{[a,b]}x\rVert\le\lVert x-y\rVert$ for
every $y$ in the box.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Definition~\ref{def:firmn} is a coordinate sum of instances of \eqref{eq:firm}; each summand is
Theorem~\ref{thm:firm} in coordinate $i$. For the distance claim, sum the coordinatewise
consequence $(x_i-Px_i)^2\le(x_i-y_i)^2$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:var}.
\end{proof}

Thus firmness of the median is \emph{not} a one-dimensional accident, whereas closure of
firmness under composition \emph{is}.

\section{Perturbing every seed at once}\label{sec:linf}

\begin{lemma}[Joint monotonicity]\label{lem:mono3}
If $a_1\le a_2$, $b_1\le b_2$, $c_1\le c_2$ then $\med(a_1,b_1,c_1)\le\med(a_2,b_2,c_2)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\med(a,b,c)=\max\bigl(\min(a,b),\min(\max(a,b),c)\bigr)$ is built from $\max$ and $\min$, each
monotone in each argument.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Translation equivariance]\label{lem:shift}
$\med(a+d,b+d,c+d)=\med(a,b,c)+d$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$t\mapsto t+d$ is an order isomorphism of $\R$, and the median commutes with order isomorphisms.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$\ell^\infty$-nonexpansiveness of the median]\label{thm:linf}
$\bigl|\med(a,b,c)-\med(a',b',c')\bigr|\le\max\bigl(|a-a'|,|b-b'|,|c-c'|\bigr)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $d$ be the right-hand side; then $a'\le a+d$, $b'\le b+d$, $c'\le c+d$, so
Lemmas~\ref{lem:mono3} and \ref{lem:shift} give $\med(a',b',c')\le\med(a,b,c)+d$; symmetrically
for the reverse.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Every rung of the quota ladder is $\ell^\infty$-$1$-Lipschitz]\label{thm:ladder}
If $|K_i-K'_i|\le d$ for all $i$ and $m\le|\iota|$, then $|Q(K,m)-Q(K',m)|\le d$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Two structural facts suffice. \emph{Pointwise monotonicity:} $K_i\le K'_i$ for all $i$ implies
$Q(K,m)\le Q(K',m)$, because the index set certifying any budget only grows. \emph{Shift bound:}
$Q(K+d,m)\le Q(K,m)+d$, because the indices certifying $Q(K,m)$ under $K$ certify $Q(K,m)+d$
under $K+d$. Chaining, $Q(K,m)\le Q(K'+d,m)\le Q(K',m)+d$, and symmetrically.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:ladder} shows Lipschitz robustness does \emph{not} distinguish rungs: maximum,
median and conservative low tail are all $1$-Lipschitz. What distinguishes them is the
\emph{breakdown point} --- $0$ for the maximum, $1/2$ for the median. Small-perturbation
stability and gross-error resistance are independent invariants.
\end{remark}

\begin{example}
For the ensemble $\{256,224,160\}$ with median $224$: if a full re-run moves every seed by at
most one grid step of $32$, the reported knee moves by at most $32$.
\end{example}

\section{Iterating the median filter}\label{sec:iter}

Fix $a\le b$, $P=P_{[a,b]}$ and $\lambda\in(0,1]$, and set
$T_\lambda(x):=(1-\lambda)x+\lambda Px$.

\begin{lemma}[Relaxation does not change the projection]\label{lem:relaxfix}
For $0\le\lambda\le1$, $P(T_\lambda x)=Px$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$T_\lambda x$ lies between $x$ and $Px$. If $x\le Px$ then $x\le T_\lambda x\le Px$, and applying
the monotone $P$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:basic}(1)) sandwiches $P(T_\lambda x)$ between $Px$ and
$P(Px)=Px$. The case $Px\le x$ is symmetric.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact geometric decay]\label{thm:decay}
For $0\le\lambda\le1$ and all $n\ge0$, $T_\lambda^{\,n}(x)-Px=(1-\lambda)^n(x-Px)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $n$, using the identity $T_\lambda z-Pz=(1-\lambda)(z-Pz)$ at $z=T_\lambda^{\,n}x$
together with $P z=Px$ from (iterated) Lemma~\ref{lem:relaxfix}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Krasnoselskii--Mann with an exact rate]
For $0<\lambda\le1$, $T_\lambda^{\,n}(x)\to Px$, with error exactly $(1-\lambda)^n|x-Px|$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Unrelaxed convergence on the line]\label{thm:unrelaxed}
Let $T:\R\to\R$ be firmly nonexpansive with at least one fixed point $p$. Then for every $x$ the
orbit $u_n=T^nx$ converges to a fixed point of $T$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:monolip}, $T$ is monotone, $1$-Lipschitz and hence continuous. Suppose
$p\le x$. Monotonicity and $Tp=p$ give $p\le u_n$ for all $n$, so $[p,\infty)$ is invariant; the
Lipschitz bound on the pair $p\le u_n$ gives $u_{n+1}-p\le u_n-p$, so the orbit is nonincreasing
and bounded below. Order completeness of $\R$ supplies $q=\inf_n u_n$ as its limit, and
continuity with $u_{n+1}=Tu_n$ forces $Tq=q$. The case $x\le p$ is symmetric.
\end{proof}

The proof is order-theoretic, not metric, and no rate is available --- nor can one be, since the
identity is firmly nonexpansive.

\begin{theorem}[Fixed points of compositions]\label{thm:fixcomp}
Let $S,T:\R\to\R$ be firmly nonexpansive with a common fixed point $p$. If $S(Tq)=q$ then $Tq=q$
and $Sq=q$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Assume $p\le q$. Monotonicity of $T$ gives $p\le Tq$ and the Lipschitz bound gives $Tq\le q$.
Applying the Lipschitz bound of $S$ to $p\le Tq$ with $Sp=p$ and $S(Tq)=q$ gives $q-p\le Tq-p$,
so $q\le Tq$. Hence $Tq=q$ and $Sq=q$. The case $q\le p$ is symmetric.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Alternating median filters converge to a consensus]\label{thm:alt}
Let $[a,b]\cap[c,d]\ne\emptyset$. For every $x$, the iteration
$x_{n+1}=P_{[a,b]}\bigl(P_{[c,d]}(x_n)\bigr)$ converges, and its limit lies in
$[a,b]\cap[c,d]$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both projections are firmly nonexpansive (Theorem~\ref{thm:firm}) and fix any point $p$ of the
intersection (Proposition~\ref{prop:basic}(3)). Their composite is firmly nonexpansive by
Theorem~\ref{thm:comp} and fixes $p$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:unrelaxed} gives convergence to a fixed
point $q$ of the composite; Theorem~\ref{thm:fixcomp} upgrades $q$ to a common fixed point, and
points fixed by $P_{[a,b]}$ lie in $[a,b]$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:fixcomp} is what rules out a spurious compromise. A single unrelaxed median
filter stabilises in one step, $P^{\,n+1}=P$; iteration is interesting only for compositions.

\begin{example}
With brackets $[160,256]$ and $[224,384]$, the alternating filter started anywhere converges into
$[224,256]$: a budget simultaneously consistent with both ensembles.
\end{example}

\section{Algorithmic summary}

\textbf{A. Robust knee report ($O(1)$).} Sort three seeds and take the middle; equivalently clamp
one seed into the bracket of the other two. Theorem~\ref{thm:firm} certifies that redrawing one
seed by $\delta$ changes the report by at most $\delta$, with output shift and residual shift in a
disc of radius $\delta$ (on its boundary when the two seed values fall on the same side of the
bracket).

\textbf{B. Ensemble grid-stability certificate ($O(n)$).} Compute $d=\lVert K-K'\rVert_\infty$;
Theorem~\ref{thm:ladder} certifies every rung of the quota ladder differs by at most $d$.

\textbf{C. Relaxed median tracking ($O(1)$ per step).} Apply
$x\mapsto(1-\lambda)x+\lambda\med(x,a,b)$; Theorem~\ref{thm:decay} gives the exact error
$(1-\lambda)^n|x_0-\med|$, so the step count to tolerance $\varepsilon$ is known in advance.

\textbf{D. Two-ensemble consensus ($O(1)$ per step).} Alternate the two clamps;
Theorem~\ref{thm:alt} guarantees convergence into the intersection. On the line convergence is in
fact finite; the theorem's value is that it needs no overlap detection and extends to longer
chains of filters.

\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{Why ``firm'' rather than ``nonexpansive''.} The practitioner's rule of thumb is
Corollary~\ref{cor:ne}: true but lossy. The exact statement is the quadratic budget of
Theorem~\ref{thm:firm}, and the extra term is not decoration --- Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} exhibits
a nonexpansive map with the same range and the same fixed-point set as the median that
nevertheless mis-reports the direction of an outlier.

\paragraph{Two independent axes of robustness.} All rungs of the ladder share Lipschitz constant
$1$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:ladder}), so the classical hierarchy among order statistics is entirely a
breakdown-point phenomenon.

\paragraph{Dimension.} Firmness survives the passage to $\R^n$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:box}) because
the coordinatewise median \emph{is} a box projection; composition-closure does not
(Theorem~\ref{thm:compfail}), because it rests on the collapse of firmness to an order condition.
Chained median filters are therefore unconditionally well-behaved on scalar streams, while
multidimensional chains require the standard averaged-operator theory.

\paragraph{Relation to proximal algorithms.} By Theorem~\ref{thm:unique} the median is
$\prox_{\iota_{[a,b]}}$ and by Theorem~\ref{thm:refl} it is the $\tfrac12$-average of the identity
with $2P-I$; it is the resolvent of the normal-cone operator of the bracket. Every splitting
scheme admitting a projection step admits a median step, with the robust-statistics reading
attached.

\section{Future work}

\textbf{C1. Classification of ensemble selectors.} Let $F:\R^n\to\R$ be translation equivariant
($F(K+c\mathbf 1)=F(K)+c$), permutation invariant, $\ell^\infty$-$1$-Lipschitz and internal
($F(K)\in[\min K,\max K]$). These clauses do not force $F$ to be an order statistic --- medians of
sub-multisets and midranges qualify. Conjecturally, adding coordinatewise monotonicity and
idempotence under single-seed constant perturbations does force $F$ to be a $k$-th order
statistic. Theorem~\ref{thm:ladder} supplies the Lipschitz clause and Lemma~\ref{lem:mono3} the
monotonicity clause; Theorem~\ref{thm:monolip} is why such a classification is conceivable, since
it converts metric hypotheses into order hypotheses.

\textbf{C2. Independence of breakdown point and Lipschitz constant.} Conjecturally every
admissible pair $(\beta,L)$ is realised by some selector. Section~\ref{sec:linf} proves one half:
a whole ladder with $L=1$ and every breakdown point from $0$ to $1/2$.

\textbf{C3. Higher-dimensional characterisation.} Does the coordinatewise median admit an analogue
of Theorem~\ref{thm:char} among self-maps of $\R^n$? The order collapse is unavailable, so a
genuinely convex-analytic argument is required.

\textbf{C4. Rates for unrelaxed composites.} Under what geometric hypotheses on two brackets does
the alternating filter of Theorem~\ref{thm:alt} converge linearly, with a rate expressed in terms
of the overlap?

\textbf{C5. Median splitting schemes.} Replace projection steps in standard splitting algorithms
by median steps over ensembles of constraints, and quantify the robustness of the resulting
algorithm to corrupted constraints.

\section{Conclusion}

The median of three numbers is the metric projection onto the interval spanned by two of them.
Its robustness is therefore not merely Lipschitz but \emph{firm}: estimate displacement and
absorbed-residual displacement obey a Pythagorean budget. Firm nonexpansiveness on the line is
precisely monotonicity together with the $1$-Lipschitz bound, and this identification
characterises the median outright by three axioms, none weakenable to plain nonexpansiveness.
Firmness survives the passage to $\R^n$ while composition-closure does not; simultaneous
perturbation is controlled in $\ell^\infty$ for every rung of an order-statistic ladder, isolating
the breakdown point as the only separating invariant; and iteration --- relaxed with an exact
geometric rate, or unrelaxed by an order-theoretic completeness argument --- yields convergent
median filters that find genuine consensus between two ensembles.

